+          "claim": "Since $\\bar v=v$ $M$-almost everywhere, the distributional continuity-equation identity also holds with $\\bar v$. Let $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty((0,T)\\times\\mathbb R^d)$ and set $\\psi(t,y)=\\int\\varphi(t,x)\\rho_\\varepsilon(x-y)dx$. This function and its derivatives decay rapidly in y, but are not compactly supported. Choose smooth cutoffs $\\chi_R$ equal to one on $B_R$, supported in $B_{2R}$, with $|\\nabla\\chi_R|\\le C/R$, and apply the equation with $\\bar v$ to $\\chi_R(y)\\psi(t,y)$. As $R\\to\\infty$, dominated convergence [F5] sends the terms with $\\chi_R$ to their uncut values: $\\partial_t\\psi$ and $\\nabla_y\\psi$ are bounded, while $|v|$ is integrable against M by [F1]. The extra term $\\psi v\\cdot\\nabla\\chi_R$ tends to zero, bounded in absolute integral by $C\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty R^{-1}\\int|v|dM$. Thus $\\int(\\partial_t\\psi+\\nabla_y\\psi\\cdot v_t(y))d\\mu_t(y)dt=0.$ Differentiating the convolution and integrating by parts in x gives $\\nabla_y\\psi(t,y)=\\int\\nabla_x\\varphi(t,x)\\rho_\\varepsilon(x-y)dx$. Fubini then rewrites the last identity as $\\int_0^T\\int\\left(\\partial_t\\varphi(t,x)p_t^\\varepsilon(x)+\\nabla_x\\varphi(t,x)\\cdot j_t^\\varepsilon(x)\\right)dxdt=0,$ which is exactly the continuity equation for density $p_t^\\varepsilon$ and velocity $v_t^\\varepsilon=j_t^\\varepsilon/p_t^\\varepsilon$.",
