-          "claim": "Assume the spatial derivative hypothesis. On a short interval and compact ball as in step 1.1, solutions from nearby initial points remain in a common compact ball. The contraction estimate gives $\\|X(\\cdot;s,x_0+h)-X(\\cdot;s,x_0)\\|_\\infty\\le C|h|$. Write $\\Delta_h(t)=X(t;s,x_0+h)-X(t;s,x_0)$. The state-variable fundamental theorem of calculus [F3] gives $\\Delta_h(t)=h+\\int_s^t A_h(q)\\Delta_h(q)dq,\\quad A_h(q)=\\int_0^1D_xb(q,X(q;s,x_0)+\\theta\\Delta_h(q))d\\theta.$ The matrix equation $V(t)=I_d+\\int_s^tA(q)V(q)dq$ has a unique continuous solution by the contraction argument of step 1.1, since $\\int_JL_K<1$. Subtracting its product with h from the equation for $\\Delta_h$ gives $\\Delta_h(t)-V(t)h=\\int_s^t A_h(q)(\\Delta_h(q)-V(q)h)dq+\\int_s^t(A_h(q)-A(q))V(q)h\\,dq.$ Taking sup norms and absorbing the first term bounds the left side by $C|h|\\int_J\\|A_h-A\\|dq=o(|h|)$. Thus $D_{x_0}X=V$; dominated convergence in the same integral equation proves continuity of V in $x_0$. The integral FTC [F2] yields $\\dot V=A(t)V$ almost everywhere. Repeating on a finite cover by overlapping short intervals gives $C^1$ dependence throughout each common flow domain and continuous dependence in the uniform path norm on trajectories existing through the full interval.",
+          "claim": "Assume the spatial derivative hypothesis. On a short interval and compact ball as in step 1.1, solutions from nearby initial points remain in a common compact ball. The contraction estimate gives $\\|X(\\cdot;s,x_0+h)-X(\\cdot;s,x_0)\\|_\\infty\\le C|h|$. Write $\\Delta_h(t)=X(t;s,x_0+h)-X(t;s,x_0)$. The state-variable fundamental theorem of calculus [F3] gives $\\Delta_h(t)=h+\\int_s^t A_h(q)\\Delta_h(q)dq,\\quad A_h(q)=\\int_0^1D_xb(q,X(q;s,x_0)+\\theta\\Delta_h(q))d\\theta.$ Put $A(q)=D_xb(q,X(q;s,x_0))$ at the almost-everywhere times where the spatial differential exists, with value zero on its exceptional time set. The matrix equation $V(t)=I_d+\\int_s^tA(q)V(q)dq$ has a unique continuous solution by the contraction argument of step 1.1, since $\\int_JL_K<1$. Subtracting its product with h from the equation for $\\Delta_h$ gives $\\Delta_h(t)-V(t)h=\\int_s^t A_h(q)(\\Delta_h(q)-V(q)h)dq+\\int_s^t(A_h(q)-A(q))V(q)h\\,dq.$ Taking sup norms and absorbing the first term bounds the left side by $C|h|\\int_J\\|A_h-A\\|dq=o(|h|)$. Thus $D_{x_0}X=V$; dominated convergence in the same integral equation proves continuity of V in $x_0$. The integral FTC [F2] yields $\\dot V=A(t)V$ almost everywhere. Repeating on a finite cover by overlapping short intervals gives $C^1$ dependence throughout each common flow domain and continuous dependence in the uniform path norm on trajectories existing through the full interval.",
