-          "claim": "Fix a compact flow tube and a compact set K of states it visits. Choose a countable dense subset Q of K. By [F6], outside one null set the functions $m_K,L_K$ and $s\\mapsto b(s,q)$ for all $q\\in Q$ have their values as limits of local averages. At such times, the average of $b(s,x)$ over a shrinking interval converges to $b(s,x)$ uniformly for $x\\in K$: approximate x by q\\in Q, bound the average error by $|x-q|$ times the average of L_K, and use continuity/Lipschitz continuity of the time slice at s; then let q approach x. For a solution started at $x$ at time $s+h$, the local magnitude bound gives $|X(r;s+h,x)-x|\\le\\int_s^{s+h}m_K=O(|h|)$ uniformly over $r$ between s and s+h. Its difference from the frozen integrand $b(r,x)$ contributes at most $|h|^{-1}\\int_s^{s+h}L_K(r)O(|h|)dr=O(|h|)$ to the average, using that s is a Lebesgue point of L_K. Therefore the integral equation gives $[X(s;s+h,x)-x]/h\\to-b(s,x)$ uniformly on K. The flow identity $X(t;s+h,x)=X(t;s,X(s;s+h,x))$ and the spatial derivative from step 3.1 yield $\\partial_sX(t;s,x)=-D_xX(t;s,x)b(s,x)$ almost everywhere on the tube. The right side has an integrable local bound, so the start-time dependence is locally absolutely continuous.",
+          "claim": "Fix a compact flow tube and a compact set K of states it visits. For a fixed target time t, choose a compact time interval J containing t and the varying start times, with all trajectories in the flow identity remaining in K. For trajectories with the same starting time, the integral equation gives $|X(r;s,y)-X(r;s,z)|\\le|y-z|+\\int_JL_K(q)|X(q;s,y)-X(q;s,z)|dq$; iterating this inequality yields the spatial Lipschitz bound $|X(t;s,y)-X(t;s,z)|\\le e^{\\int_JL_K}|y-z|$. For $s,s+h\\in J$, the flow identity and the integral equation along the short backward segment give $X(t;s+h,x)=X(t;s,X(s;s+h,x))$ and $|X(s;s+h,x)-x|\\le\\int_{[s,s+h]}m_K(q)dq$, where the integral is over the unoriented interval. Hence $|X(t;s+h,x)-X(t;s,x)|\\le e^{\\int_JL_K}\\int_{[s,s+h]}m_K(q)dq$. Summing over any disjoint adjacent intervals shows that $s\\mapsto X(t;s,x)$ is locally absolutely continuous, uniformly for x in the tube. To identify its derivative, choose a countable dense subset Q of K. By [F6], outside one null set the functions $m_K,L_K$ and $s\\mapsto b(s,q)$ for all $q\\in Q$ have their values as limits of local averages; also the spatial Lipschitz bound holds at such s. Approximation by q and the common Lipschitz bound show that the averages of $b(r,x)$ over intervals shrinking to s converge to $b(s,x)$ uniformly for $x\\in K$. For the solution started at x at time $s+h$, the magnitude bound gives $|X(r;s+h,x)-x|\\le\\int_{[s,s+h]}m_K=O(|h|)$ uniformly between s and s+h at these Lebesgue points. Replacing $b(r,x)$ by $b(r,X(r;s+h,x))$ in the average contributes at most $|h|^{-1}O(|h|)\\int_{[s,s+h]}L_K(r)dr=O(|h|)$. The integral equation therefore gives $[X(s;s+h,x)-x]/h\\to-b(s,x)$ uniformly on K. The flow identity $X(t;s+h,x)=X(t;s,X(s;s+h,x))$ and the spatial derivative from step 3.1 yield $\\partial_sX(t;s,x)=-D_xX(t;s,x)b(s,x)$ almost everywhere on the tube. The right side has the integrable local bound $e^{\\int_JL_K}m_K(s)$, consistent with and also giving the local absolute continuity.",
