-          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T>0$, $d\\ge1$, and let $(\\mu_t,v_t)$ be a narrowly continuous finite-action solution of the continuity equation on $[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d$, with space-time law $M(dt,dx)=dt\\,\\mu_t(dx)$. For $\\varepsilon>0$, let $\\rho_\\varepsilon$ be the strictly positive centered Gaussian probability density with covariance $\\varepsilon I_d$, and define\n$$p_t^\\varepsilon(x)=\\int\\rho_\\varepsilon(x-y)d\\mu_t(y),\\quad j_t^\\varepsilon(x)=\\int\\rho_\\varepsilon(x-y)v_t(y)d\\mu_t(y),\\quad v_t^\\varepsilon(x)=j_t^\\varepsilon(x)/p_t^\\varepsilon(x).$$\nThen $p_t^\\varepsilon$ is a strictly positive smooth probability density for each $t$, $t\\mapsto\\mu_t^\\varepsilon$ is narrowly continuous, $v_t^\\varepsilon$ is Borel and locally Lipschitz in $x$ with local magnitude and Lipschitz bounds integrable in time, and $(\\mu_t*\\rho_\\varepsilon,v_t^\\varepsilon)$ solves the continuity equation. For almost every $t$,\n$$\\int|v_t^\\varepsilon(x)|^2p_t^\\varepsilon(x)dx\\le\\int|v_t(y)|^2d\\mu_t(y).$$",
+          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T>0$, $d\\ge1$, and let $(\\mu_t,v_t)$ be a narrowly continuous finite-action solution of the continuity equation on $[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d$, with space-time law $M(dt,dx)=dt\\,\\mu_t(dx)$. Put $a(t)=\\int|v_t(y)|^2d\\mu_t(y)$ and $N=\\{t:a(t)=+\\infty\\}$. Then $a$ is measurable, $N$ is Borel and Lebesgue-null, and the Borel representative $\\bar v(t,x)=0$ for $t\\in N$ and $\\bar v(t,x)=v(t,x)$ for $t\\notin N$ agrees with $v$ $M$-almost everywhere. For $\\varepsilon>0$, let $\\rho_\\varepsilon$ be the strictly positive centered Gaussian probability density with covariance $\\varepsilon I_d$, and define\n$$p_t^\\varepsilon(x)=\\int\\rho_\\varepsilon(x-y)d\\mu_t(y),\\quad j_t^\\varepsilon(x)=\\int\\rho_\\varepsilon(x-y)\\bar v_t(y)d\\mu_t(y),\\quad v_t^\\varepsilon(x)=j_t^\\varepsilon(x)/p_t^\\varepsilon(x).$$\nThen $p_t^\\varepsilon$ is a strictly positive smooth probability density for each $t$, $t\\mapsto\\mu_t^\\varepsilon$ is narrowly continuous, $v_t^\\varepsilon$ is Borel and locally Lipschitz in $x$ with local magnitude and Lipschitz bounds integrable in time, and $(\\mu_t*\\rho_\\varepsilon,v_t^\\varepsilon)$ solves the continuity equation. For almost every $t$,\n$$\\int|v_t^\\varepsilon(x)|^2p_t^\\varepsilon(x)dx\\le\\int|v_t(y)|^2d\\mu_t(y).$$",
