+          "claim": "For this fixed t define the restricted finite pushforward $\\nu_t$ on $E_t$ by $\\nu_t(B)=\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(\\{x\\in D_t:X_t^\\varepsilon(x)\\in B\\})$ for Borel $B\\subseteq E_t$. The set is Borel because $X_t^\\varepsilon$ is continuous on the open domain $D_t$; preimages preserve disjoint unions, so $\\nu_t$ is a finite Borel measure of mass $\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(D_t)$. The weak continuity-equation identity extends from smooth compact tests to compactly supported tests that are $C^1$ in x and absolutely continuous in r with locally integrable time derivative: multiply by a time cutoff, convolve in time and space on a fixed compact neighborhood, and pass to the limit. The terms converge because $p^\\varepsilon$ is locally bounded, $p^\\varepsilon v^\\varepsilon$ is locally integrable in time and space by finite action, and the test and its spatial gradient converge uniformly while its time derivative converges in $L^1$. Apply this extension to $\\eta(r)\\psi(r,x)$ from step 1.1 for every smooth compactly supported time cutoff $\\eta$. The transport equation leaves $\\int\\eta'(r)F(r)dr=0$, so $F(r)=\\int\\psi(r,x)d\\mu_r^\\varepsilon(x)$ is constant on $(0,t)$. Continuity of the flow and of $\\mu_r^\\varepsilon$ extends the identity to r=0,t, giving $\\int f(y)d\\mu_t^\\varepsilon(y)=\\int f(X_t^\\varepsilon(x))d\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(x)$ for every $f\\in C_c^\\infty(E_t)$. Uniform mollification extends equality to every $f\\in C_c(E_t)$; both measures are finite, so uniform limits pass through their integrals. For a compact $K\\subset E_t$, choose n sufficiently large that the closed $1/n$-neighborhood of K is compactly contained in $E_t$ and set $f_n(x)=\\max\\{0,1-n\\operatorname{dist}(x,K)\\}$. Then $f_n\\in C_c(E_t)$ and $f_n\\downarrow\\mathbf1_K$, so [F6] gives $\\nu_t(K)=\\mu_t^\\varepsilon(K)$. By [F5], both finite Borel measures on the Polish space $E_t$ are inner regular; their agreement on compact sets therefore proves $\\nu_t=\\mu_t^\\varepsilon|_{E_t}$.",
