-          "claim": "Let $\\tau(x)$ be the maximal forward existence time. The measure identity of step 2.1, first for bounded nonnegative truncations of $|v_t^\\varepsilon|^2$ and then by monotone convergence, gives $\\int\\!\\int_0^{\\min\\{T,\\tau(x)\\}}|v_r^\\varepsilon(X_r^\\varepsilon(x))|^2dr\\,d\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(x)\\le\\int_0^T\\!\\int|v_r^\\varepsilon(y)|^2d\\mu_r^\\varepsilon(y)dr<\\infty.$ Tonelli [F4] shows that for $\\mu_0^\\varepsilon$-almost every x the path has finite squared speed integral up to its maximal endpoint. Holder then makes its total path length finite. Such a path is Cauchy at any finite endpoint; it cannot escape every compact set, contradicting [F2] if an endpoint lies in $(0,T)$. At T it has a finite limit and extends continuously to T. Thus $\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(D_T)=1$.",
+          "claim": "For every $r\\in(0,T]$, step 2.1 gives equality of the restricted finite measures on $E_r$. Applying it to $y\\mapsto\\min\\{N,|v_r^\\varepsilon(y)|^2\\}$ and letting $N\\uparrow\\infty$ by [F7] yields $\\int_{D_r}|v_r^\\varepsilon(X_r^\\varepsilon(x))|^2d\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(x)=\\int_{E_r}|v_r^\\varepsilon(y)|^2d\\mu_r^\\varepsilon(y)\\le\\int|v_r^\\varepsilon(y)|^2d\\mu_r^\\varepsilon(y)$. Let $\\mathscr D=\\{(r,x):x\\in D_r\\}$. This domain is relatively open in $[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d$ and $(r,x)\\mapsto X_r^\\varepsilon(x)$ is continuous on it: cover any compact trajectory segment through $(r,x)$ by finitely many local flow neighborhoods from [F2] and patch their continuous dependence on the initial point; time continuity follows from the integral equation. Thus the integrand is Borel; extend it by zero off $\\mathscr D$. Tonelli [F4] then gives $\\int\\!\\int_0^{\\min\\{T,\\tau(x)\\}}|v_r^\\varepsilon(X_r^\\varepsilon(x))|^2dr\\,d\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(x)\\le\\int_0^T\\!\\int|v_r^\\varepsilon(y)|^2d\\mu_r^\\varepsilon(y)dr<\\infty.$ Thus for $\\mu_0^\\varepsilon$-almost every x the path has finite squared speed integral up to its maximal endpoint. Holder then makes its total path length finite. Such a path is Cauchy at any finite endpoint; it cannot escape every compact set, contradicting [F2] if an endpoint lies in $(0,T)$. At T it has a finite limit and extends continuously to T. Thus $\\mu_0^\\varepsilon(D_T)=1$.",
